### The problem hexagonal architecture is solving in a Nest codebase specifically

The default NestJS tutorial style couples business logic directly to the framework: a `@Injectable()` service with `@InjectRepository(Order)` injected straight in, business rules living alongside ORM calls in the same method. This works fine for CRUD apps, but for a domain with real business rules, it means you can't unit test a pricing rule without spinning up (or heavily mocking) TypeORM, and swapping the ORM or adding a second delivery mechanism (a CLI, a gRPC endpoint) means touching the business logic itself. Hexagonal architecture's core move: the domain layer defines **ports** (interfaces) for what it needs from the outside world, and framework-specific code lives entirely in **adapters** that implement those ports — the domain never imports anything from `@nestjs/*` or `typeorm`.

```typescript
// domain/order.ts — pure TypeScript, zero framework imports
export class Order {
  private constructor(public readonly id: string, private items: OrderItem[], private status: OrderStatus) {}

  static create(id: string, items: OrderItem[]): Order {
    if (items.length === 0) throw new EmptyOrderError();
    return new Order(id, items, OrderStatus.Draft);
  }

  ship(): void {
    if (this.status !== OrderStatus.Paid) throw new InvalidOrderStateError('Cannot ship unpaid order');
    this.status = OrderStatus.Shipped;
  }
}

// domain/ports/order-repository.port.ts — the port: an interface the domain depends on
export interface OrderRepositoryPort {
  save(order: Order): Promise<void>;
  findById(id: string): Promise<Order | null>;
}
export const ORDER_REPOSITORY = Symbol('ORDER_REPOSITORY'); // DI token, still framework-agnostic as a plain symbol

// application/create-order.use-case.ts — orchestration, still no Nest decorators required to be testable
export class CreateOrderUseCase {
  constructor(private readonly orders: OrderRepositoryPort) {} // depends on the interface, not TypeORM
  async execute(id: string, items: OrderItem[]): Promise<void> {
    const order = Order.create(id, items);
    await this.orders.save(order);
  }
}
```

### Where NestJS actually enters the picture

The **adapter** layer is where `@Injectable()`, `@InjectRepository()`, and everything Nest-specific live — it implements the port using framework tools, and is wired via a custom provider so the DI token stays the abstract port symbol, never the concrete class:

```typescript
// infrastructure/typeorm-order.repository.ts — adapter: implements the port using TypeORM
@Injectable()
export class TypeOrmOrderRepository implements OrderRepositoryPort {
  constructor(@InjectRepository(OrderEntity) private repo: Repository<OrderEntity>) {}
  async save(order: Order): Promise<void> { /* map domain Order -> OrderEntity, repo.save(...) */ }
  async findById(id: string): Promise<Order | null> { /* repo.findOne, map back to domain Order */ }
}

@Module({
  providers: [
    CreateOrderUseCase, // Nest instantiates this fine even though it's not decorated — see pitfall below
    { provide: ORDER_REPOSITORY, useClass: TypeOrmOrderRepository },
  ],
})
export class OrdersModule {}
```

This buys you two concrete things: `CreateOrderUseCase` and `Order` are testable with plain Jest, zero Nest testing module, zero DB — construct a fake `OrderRepositoryPort` by hand; and swapping TypeORM for Prisma, or adding a second inbound adapter (a Kafka consumer alongside the HTTP controller) touches only the adapter layer, never the domain.

**Common pitfall:** Letting DTOs with `class-validator` decorators (`@IsString()`, `@IsNotEmpty()`) leak into the domain layer as if they were domain objects. `class-validator` decorators are a framework/transport concern — validating what came over HTTP — and belong on request DTOs in the adapter layer; a domain entity should enforce its invariants through its own constructor/factory logic (as `Order.create()` does above), not by importing validation decorators, or the "pure domain" layer quietly becomes coupled to NestJS's HTTP validation pipeline again.

