### What a trace gives you that scattered logs across services never can

A correlation ID in log lines lets you `grep` for related entries across services, but reconstructing the actual call tree — which service called which, how long each hop took, where the time actually went across five hops — from grep output is manual reconstruction. A **trace** is that call tree as structured data: a root span for the initial request, child spans for every downstream call, each with start/end timestamps, forming a tree you can visualize (in Jaeger, Tempo, etc.) and immediately see which specific hop in a 5-service chain added 400ms.

```typescript
// tracing.ts — instrumented once at bootstrap, not per-request
const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  traceExporter: new OTLPTraceExporter({ url: 'http://otel-collector:4318/v1/traces' }),
  instrumentations: [getNodeAutoInstrumentations()], // auto-instruments http, express, some DB clients
});
sdk.start();
```

Auto-instrumentation covers HTTP and common libraries out of the box, but Nest's microservice transports (TCP, Kafka `ClientProxy`) aren't standard HTTP, so they need explicit span creation and context propagation at the transport boundary — this is the part that actually requires understanding, not just installing a package.

### How context crosses a TCP or Kafka boundary

A trace's identity (trace ID + parent span ID) has to travel *with* the message, since the receiving service has no other way to know which trace it's part of. OpenTelemetry's convention is to inject this into message headers/metadata using the W3C `traceparent` format, and extract it on the receiving side before creating a child span:

```typescript
// sending side — inject trace context into the message metadata
async callInventory(payload: any) {
  const span = tracer.startSpan('inventory.reserve');
  const carrier: Record<string, string> = {};
  propagation.inject(trace.setSpan(context.active(), span), carrier); // writes traceparent into carrier

  return firstValueFrom(
    this.inventoryClient.send('reserve-inventory', { ...payload, _trace: carrier }),
  ).finally(() => span.end());
}

// receiving side — extract and continue the same trace as a child span
@MessagePattern('reserve-inventory')
handleReserve(@Payload() data: any) {
  const parentContext = propagation.extract(context.active(), data._trace);
  return context.with(parentContext, () => {
    const span = tracer.startSpan('inventory.handle-reserve'); // child of the sender's span
    try {
      return this.inventoryService.reserve(data);
    } finally {
      span.end();
    }
  });
}
```

Without this explicit inject/extract at every non-HTTP transport hop, each service's spans exist in isolation — you get per-service traces that never link into one end-to-end tree, which defeats the entire point of distributed tracing (you're back to manually correlating disconnected fragments, just with fancier UI per fragment).

### What spans encode that a log line structurally can't

A span has a duration (start and end, not a single timestamp), a parent-child relationship to other spans, and structured attributes (`db.statement`, `http.status_code`) queryable across the whole trace set — this is what lets you ask "show me every trace where the `inventory.reserve` span took over 200ms" as a query, rather than eyeballing timestamps across scattered log lines and doing the subtraction by hand.

**Common pitfall:** Instrumenting only inbound HTTP controllers and assuming the trace is complete, while every outbound `ClientProxy.send()` to another microservice silently starts a brand-new, disconnected trace on the receiving side because context propagation was never wired into the transport layer. The dashboard then shows dozens of tiny isolated one-hop traces per request instead of the single coherent end-to-end trace the whole investment in tracing was meant to produce.

